"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][3]={"school_physics_en:M3-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M3-L1 – Sound sources and vibrations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Sound and acoustics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Why can we hear a tuning fork even when its vibration is barely\nvisible? A <strong>sound source<\/strong> is an object that makes its\nsurroundings vibrate mechanically. Examples include a tuning fork, a\nguitar string, a drum membrane and the vibrating air column in a wind\ninstrument. A single movement may create a brief sound pulse; regular\nvibrations can produce a tone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How sound is produced and transmitted<\/h2>\n<p>A vibrating tuning fork alternately compresses nearby air and allows\nit to expand. It creates regions of <strong>higher<\/strong> and\n<strong>lower pressure<\/strong> (compressions and rarefactions) that\npropagate. Individual air particles mainly oscillate about their\nequilibrium positions; they do not travel from the fork all the way to\nthe ear.<\/p>\n<p>In air, audible sound is approximately a <strong>longitudinal\nwave<\/strong>: the motion of air particles is parallel to the direction\nof wave propagation. Solids can carry other types of mechanical waves as\nwell. Sound requires a medium; a mechanical sound wave cannot propagate\nthrough an ideal vacuum.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> A tuning fork and\nschematic compressions/rarefactions; separate arrows distinguish wave\npropagation from local particle motion.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_013_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A vibrating tuning fork creates compressions and rarefactions in air. Arrows distinguish the propagation of the disturbance from local particle motion.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A vibrating tuning fork creates\ncompressions and rarefactions in air. Arrows distinguish the propagation\nof the disturbance from local particle motion.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Sound transfers a pressure disturbance through a medium;\nparticles remain near their equilibrium positions.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Applying the vocabulary<\/h2>\n<p>A ruler projects over the edge of a table. If its free end is\ndisplaced gently and released, it vibrates. Distinguish:\n<strong>source<\/strong> = ruler, <strong>vibration<\/strong> =\nback-and-forth motion, <strong>sound wave<\/strong> = travelling\ndisturbance in the medium, <strong>receiver<\/strong> = ear or\nmicrophone. The entire ruler is not carried towards the ear.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe investigation<\/h2>\n<p>Hold a plastic ruler against a tabletop and gently vibrate its free\nend. Compare a short and a long overhanging section. Observe without\nallowing the ruler to strike people or objects. Do not produce loud\ntones or test loudspeakers directly next to the ear.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Identify the source, medium and receiver for a struck tuning\nfork.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is “the air travels as sound from the speaker to the ear”\nincorrect?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How do particles vibrate relative to the propagation direction in an\nideal longitudinal wave?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can sound travel in the ideal vacuum of space? Explain.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Source: tuning fork; medium: for example air; receiver: ear or\nmicrophone.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The disturbance and energy are transmitted; air particles mainly\noscillate locally.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Parallel to the direction of propagation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No: the material medium needed to transmit the mechanical\ndisturbance is missing.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limits and next step<\/h2>\n<p>The regular compression pattern is an idealized harmonic wave; real\nnoises often contain many superimposed vibrations. Next we calculate <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">sound travel times<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Portal – Physics (grades 7–10), official curriculum and\nmaterials<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Topics/teaching materials on sound and speed of sound ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/17-1-sound-waves\">OpenStax\n– University Physics Volume 1, 17.1 Sound Waves<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Longitudinal\nwaves, compressions and rarefactions · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/persoenliche-schutzausruestung\">Sichere\nSchule – Personal protective equipment (hearing protection)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · School\nexperiments, risk assessment, hearing protection · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M3-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M3-L2 – Speed of sound and sound transmission<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Sound and acoustics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>We see a distant firework flash before hearing the bang. At these\ndistances light travel time is negligible, but sound takes a measurable\namount of time. The <strong>speed of sound<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntells us how far a sound disturbance travels per unit time in a\nparticular medium.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Speed in a medium<\/h2>\n<p>For a sound front travelling at approximately constant speed,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=c\\,t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=s/c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn still air at about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsound travels at roughly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>343<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">343\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nwe use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>340<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">340\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor approximate calculations. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is slower, around\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>331<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">331\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSound is typically faster in water or many solids. The precise value\ndepends on material properties and conditions, not simply on density\nalone.<\/p>\n<p>A material medium transmits the pressure disturbance. <strong>Wave\nspeed<\/strong> is not the same as <strong>particle speed<\/strong>: the\ndisturbance moves through the material although individual particles\nmake only short movements.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> On a distance–time plot,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorresponds to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>170<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">170\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.0\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>340<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">340\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(model value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>340<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=340\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_014_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A linear plot from 0 metres at 0 seconds to 170 metres at 0.5 seconds and 340 metres at 1 second for c = 340 metres per second.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A linear plot from 0 metres at 0 seconds\nto 170 metres at 0.5 seconds and 340 metres at 1 second for c = 340\nmetres per second.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>At constant speed, the distance travelled by a sound wave grows\nin proportion to time.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A noise source is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>204<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">204\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom an observer. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>340<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=340\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>s<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>204<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>340<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\frac{s}{c}=\\frac{204\\,\\mathrm{m}}{340\\,\\mathrm{m/s}}=0.60\\,\\mathrm{s}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This is the <strong>one-way path<\/strong>. A returning echo travels\nto the reflecting surface and back.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe investigation<\/h2>\n<p>Make a soft tapping sound on a table and notice how difficult short\ndelays are to measure with a handheld stopwatch. For more accurate work,\nuse an existing sound recording or two microphones under guidance; do\nnot use firecrackers, bursting balloons or other loud impulses.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the travel time for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>68<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">68\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>340<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=340\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How far does a sound front travel in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat that speed?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does speaking more loudly not simply increase\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin air at a fixed temperature?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why should a precise experiment record air temperature?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>68<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>340<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=68/340=0.20\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>340<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>510<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=340\\cdot1.5=510\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The propagation speed of small disturbances is primarily set by the\nmedium, not loudness.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Sound speed in air changes with temperature.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limits and next step<\/h2>\n<p>The equation assumes one travel path under approximately constant\nconditions. Wind, temperature gradients and obstacles can affect real\nmeasurements. Next we study <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">frequency and pressure\namplitude<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Portal – Physics (grades 7–10), official curriculum and\nmaterials<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Topics/teaching materials on sound and speed of sound ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/14-1-speed-of-sound-frequency-and-wavelength\">OpenStax\n– Physics, 14.1 Speed of Sound, Frequency, and Wavelength<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sound\nspeed in media and v=fλ · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/17-2-speed-of-sound-frequency-and-wavelength\">OpenStax\n– College Physics 2e, 17.2 Speed of Sound, Frequency, and Wavelength<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· Travel time and echo; temperature dependence · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M3-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M3-L3 – Pitch, frequency and loudness<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Sound and acoustics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A high-pitched tone need not be loud, and a low-pitched tone need not\nbe quiet. <strong>Pitch<\/strong> and <strong>loudness<\/strong> describe\ndifferent perceptual properties. A sound graph lets us distinguish the\nrate of repetition from the strength of the pressure variation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Frequency, period and amplitude<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Frequency<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the number of complete cycles per second and is measured in hertz\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nThe <strong>period<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the time taken for one cycle. They are related by:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>T<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>f<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=\\frac{1}{T},\\qquad T=\\frac{1}{f}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For a simple periodic tone, a higher frequency generally corresponds\nto a higher perceived pitch. <strong>Sound-pressure amplitude<\/strong>,\nby contrast, is the maximum magnitude of a pressure variation from the\nequilibrium value. Under otherwise identical conditions, greater\npressure amplitude means greater sound intensity. Perceived\n<em>loudness<\/em> also depends on frequency, duration and an\nindividual’s hearing. Sound level in <strong>decibels<\/strong> is\nlogarithmic, not a linear amplitude measure.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> The top panels have the\nsame amplitude but different frequencies; the bottom panels have the\nsame frequency but different amplitudes. These curves show\n<strong>pressure deviation versus time<\/strong>, not air particles\nmoving sideways.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_015_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four pressure-versus-time plots: equal amplitude and different cycle counts above, equal cycle counts and different amplitudes below.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four pressure-versus-time plots: equal\namplitude and different cycle counts above, equal cycle counts and\ndifferent amplitudes below.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Frequency and sound-pressure amplitude are different\nproperties.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A periodic sound trace repeats every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.004<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=0.004\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>0.004<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>250<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=\\frac{1}{0.004\\,\\mathrm{s}}=250\\,\\mathrm{Hz}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A second trace with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.002<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=0.002\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a frequency of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">500\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts frequency is twice as high; this does <strong>not<\/strong> mean it\nis perceived as twice as loud.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe investigation<\/h2>\n<p>Draw two sine waves with equal height but different numbers of cycles\nin the same time interval. Then change only their height. If you use a\ntone generator, choose a <strong>very low level<\/strong>, start muted\nand avoid testing hearing limits or prolonged headphone exposure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=100\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What changes in a pressure–time plot for a higher tone under\notherwise identical conditions?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">One trace has twice the pressure amplitude but the same frequency.\nWhat stays the same and what changes?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does doubling frequency also double loudness?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.010<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=1/100\\,\\mathrm{s}=0.010\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">More complete cycles per second, or a shorter period.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Frequency is unchanged; the pressure variation becomes larger.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Pitch and loudness are distinct perceptual properties.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limits and next step<\/h2>\n<p>Real voices and instruments include many frequency components; equal\nsound levels can also sound differently loud. Next we examine <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">echoes and the role of room\nsurfaces<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Portal – Physics (grades 7–10), official curriculum and\nmaterials<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Topics/teaching materials on sound and speed of sound ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/14-1-speed-of-sound-frequency-and-wavelength\">OpenStax\n– Physics, 14.1 Speed of Sound, Frequency, and Wavelength<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sound\nspeed in media and v=fλ · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/14-2-sound-intensity-and-sound-level\">OpenStax\n– Physics, 14.2 Sound Intensity and Sound Level<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Pressure\namplitude, intensity and logarithmic level · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M3-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M3-L4 – Echo, reflection and insulation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Sound and acoustics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Calling towards a rock face may cause you to hear your voice again\nafter a delay. This <strong>echo<\/strong> happens because a surface\nreflects some sound energy. Inside a classroom, many closely spaced\nreflections can create <strong>reverberation<\/strong> that reduces\nspeech clarity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reflection, absorption and insulation<\/h2>\n<p>A hard, smooth surface can reflect sound strongly; rough or\nstructured surfaces scatter it in different directions. In porous\nmaterials some sound energy is converted to heat through friction and\nother processes: this is <strong>absorption<\/strong>. <strong>Sound\ninsulation<\/strong>, in contrast, mainly aims to reduce sound\n<em>transmission through<\/em> a wall, door or window. Absorption within\na room is not the same as insulation between rooms.<\/p>\n<p>For an echo, sound must travel <strong>to<\/strong> the obstacle and\n<strong>back<\/strong>. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the one-way distance and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe total measured time, a constant sound speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2d=c\\,t,\\qquad d=\\frac{c\\,t}{2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> The echo’s outward and\nreturn journeys are shown separately on the left; on the right, incident\nsound is partly reflected, absorbed and transmitted. Arrow sizes do not\nrepresent measured proportions.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_016_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Left: reflected sound paths between source and wall. Right: an incident disturbance is partly reflected, transmitted and absorbed.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Left: reflected sound paths between\nsource and wall. Right: an incident disturbance is partly reflected,\ntransmitted and absorbed.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>An echo travels out and back; interfaces may reflect, transmit or\nabsorb sound.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>An echo arrives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.60\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter a call. For air at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>343<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=343\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>343<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>102.9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>103<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=\\frac{343\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\\cdot0.60\\,\\mathrm{s}}{2}=102.9\\,\\mathrm{m}\\approx103\\,\\mathrm{m}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Multiplying by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithout <strong>dividing by two<\/strong> incorrectly gives the total\npath length instead of the distance to the wall.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe investigation<\/h2>\n<p>Compare how clearly quiet speech can be understood in furnished and\nunfurnished rooms, without increasing the volume. List the soft and hard\nsurfaces present. One subjective impression cannot establish a\nstandardized sound-insulation rating.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An echo returns after\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.40\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>340<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=340\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is there a factor of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the echo equation?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does an acoustic curtain inside a room differ from a well-sealed\nsoundproof door?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does every reflection produce a separately audible echo?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>340<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>68<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=340\\cdot0.40/2=68\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The measured time includes <strong>both<\/strong> outward and return\ntravel.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The curtain can absorb sound and reduce reverberation; the door aims\nto block transmission between rooms.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Many reflections merge and are perceived as reverberation.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limits and next step<\/h2>\n<p>How much a surface reflects, absorbs or transmits depends on\nfrequency and construction. A single ray path is only a model. Next we\nassess <a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">noise and effective hearing\nprotection<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/17-2-speed-of-sound-frequency-and-wavelength\">OpenStax\n– College Physics 2e, 17.2 Speed of Sound, Frequency, and Wavelength<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· Travel time and echo; temperature dependence · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/technik/bauliche-anforderungen/akustik\">Sichere\nSchule – Acoustics and noise protection<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Room acoustics, absorption\nand insulation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Portal – Physics (grades 7–10), official curriculum and\nmaterials<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Topics/teaching materials on sound and speed of sound ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M3-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M3-L5 – Hearing, noise and hearing protection<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Sound and acoustics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Sound is not necessarily harmless because it comes from music or a\nclassroom experiment. Hearing responds to pressure changes, and the risk\ndepends on <strong>sound level<\/strong>, <strong>duration<\/strong> and\nhow often exposure occurs. Extremely loud single events can also be\ndangerous.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From sound to perception<\/h2>\n<p>The outer ear gathers sound, which reaches the\n<strong>eardrum<\/strong> through the ear canal. The ossicles pass the\nvibration into the inner ear. In the <strong>cochlea<\/strong>, sensory\ncells convert mechanical motion into nerve signals processed by the\nbrain. Permanent damage to sensitive structures can impair hearing.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Sound level<\/strong> is measured on a logarithmic scale.\nEnvironmental noise is commonly assessed using the A-weighted level\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_{\\mathrm{A}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{dB(A)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA particular value is <strong>not a universal threshold<\/strong> that\nguarantees safety for all people and all exposure times. According to\nNIDCD, long or repeated exposure at around\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>85<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">85\\,\\mathrm{dB(A)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand above can contribute to hearing damage.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> On the left, a highly\nsimplified pathway from sound to nerve response; on the right, a\nhierarchy of measures: <strong>quieter source – distance/transmission\npath – personal hearing protection<\/strong>. This is not a medical\nanatomy illustration.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_017_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four simplified hearing stages from outer ear to cochlea and three protective approaches from reducing source volume through distance to ear protection.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four simplified hearing stages from outer\near to cochlea and three protective approaches from reducing source\nvolume through distance to ear protection.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Simplified hearing pathway; controlling sound begins at the\nsource.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Case study: level and duration<\/h2>\n<p>For <strong>occupational continuous exposure<\/strong>, NIOSH\nrecommends a reference value of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>85<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">85\\,\\mathrm{dB(A)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover eight hours. Under its <strong>3 dB exchange model<\/strong>,\nrecommended exposure time halves for each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{dB}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincrease: for example eight hours at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>85<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">85\\,\\mathrm{dB(A)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand four hours at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>88<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">88\\,\\mathrm{dB(A)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese are <strong>preventive occupational recommendations<\/strong>, not\npersonal safety guarantees and not an invitation to test such levels.\nClassroom experiments require an appropriate risk assessment and\napplicable safety rules.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe investigation<\/h2>\n<p>Make a table of everyday <em>possible<\/em> noise sources, estimated\nexposure time and possible protective measures. An uncalibrated\nsmartphone can offer only a rough indication. Do <strong>not generate\nextra noise<\/strong> just to measure it. Persistent hearing problems\ndeserve professional medical advice.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Put eardrum, ossicles and cochlea in the correct order.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is level alone insufficient to assess exposure risk?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Under the cited NIOSH model, what time corresponds to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>91<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">91\\,\\mathrm{dB(A)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>85<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">85\\,\\mathrm{dB(A)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorresponds to eight hours?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name two protective actions to consider before relying on\nearplugs.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Eardrum → ossicles → cochlea.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Exposure depends on duration and repetition as well as level.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two increments of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{dB}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\neight → four → <strong>two hours<\/strong> (occupational reference\nmodel).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Reduce the source volume and increase distance; also reduce time or\nsound transmission.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limits and next step<\/h2>\n<p>This outline is not an audiological examination or a substitute for\nrisk assessment. The occupational recommendations are not a table of\nschool exposure limits. The next module begins with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1 – Distinguishing temperature,\ninternal energy and heat<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.nidcd.nih.gov/health/noise-induced-hearing-loss\">NIDCD\n– Noise-Induced Hearing Loss<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Hearing damage, noise levels and\nprevention · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/persoenliche-schutzausruestung\">Sichere\nSchule – Personal protective equipment (hearing protection)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · School\nexperiments, risk assessment, hearing protection · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/bulletin/2021/cicada.html\">CDC/NIOSH –\nNoise Myths Debunked<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 3 dB exchange rate and occupational exposure\nrecommendation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/technik/bauliche-anforderungen/akustik\">Sichere\nSchule – Acoustics and noise protection<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Room acoustics, absorption\nand insulation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
